1. What It Is
A provenance-aware, versioned system that takes all the evidence for a claim from multiple sources and produces a structured summary of the strength of evidence. A claim is a statement about the world. The evidence for it is scattered across studies, datasets, replications, critiques, and meta-analyses. The Evidence Aggregator collects that evidence, evaluates its quality, weights it, and produces an aggregate assessment. It is the meta-analytic layer of the suite.

It reuses:

the Physics Knowledge Library (App 23)

the Full-Pipeline Simulator (App 9)

the Null-Model Protocol Library (App 18)

the Numerical Verification Tool (App 22)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the PROV-O Exporter (App 3)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Effective-Null Builder (App 11)

the Replication Tracker (App 26)

the Anomaly Register (App 25)

the Medical Claim Verifier (App 40)

the Psychological Effect Registry (App 41)

the Climate Attribution Registry (App 42)

the Evidence Aggregator (App 46)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for reviewers, meta-analysts, decision-makers, and anyone who has to weigh evidence from multiple sources.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept a claim from a user or from another app in the suite.

Collect the evidence for the claim from one or more sources.

Store the collected evidence with its source, its type, its quality, and its provenance.

Evaluate the quality of each piece of evidence: high, moderate, low, very_low, or not_assessed.

Weight the evidence by its quality, its sample size, its effect size, and its independence from other evidence.

Compute an aggregate strength of evidence: strong, moderate, weak, or inconclusive.

Compute an aggregate uncertainty: the confidence interval around the aggregate.

Detect and report the sources of heterogeneity: where the evidence disagrees and why.

Detect and report the sources of bias: publication bias, selection bias, measurement bias, and so on.

Detect and report the gaps: where the evidence is missing and what kind of evidence is needed.

Record the aggregation method: fixed_effect, random_effects, Bayesian, or other.

Record the sensitivity analyses: what happens to the aggregate under different assumptions.

Record the provenance: the sources, the collection date, the aggregation tool version.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by aggregation ID, claim, source, evidence type, quality, aggregate strength, depositor, deposit date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the claim description, the evidence summaries, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by source, by evidence type, by quality, by aggregate strength, by year.

Support exporting an aggregation as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing an aggregation from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support linking an aggregation to a Physics Knowledge Library work.

Support linking an aggregation to a Full-Pipeline Simulator result.

Support linking an aggregation to a Null-Model Protocol result.

Support linking an aggregation to a Numerical Verification Tool result.

Support linking an aggregation to a Replication Tracker record.

Support the forest plot: a visualisation of the evidence with its effect sizes and confidence intervals.

Support the funnel plot: a visualisation of publication bias.

Support the heterogeneity map: a visualisation of where the evidence agrees and disagrees.

Support the evidence quality view: a visualisation of the quality of each piece of evidence.

Support the bias assessment view: a visualisation of the sources of bias.

Support the gap analysis view: a visualisation of what evidence is missing.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical aggregation in under 100 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 aggregations.

Versioned: the system records the STAG schema version, the Null-Model Protocol schema version, the evidence aggregation schema version, and the system version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

